 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Inside each comparator",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Each comparator has a PNP Darlington input pair biased from VCC+ by 3.5 µA and 100 µA current sources, an NPN current-mirror load, and an NPN output transistor whose collector is the output and whose emitter is VCC-. The output transistor only sinks current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "On a single supply the common-mode span reaches down to the negative rail, up to VCC+ -1.5 V (VCC+ -2 V over temperature). The differential input range equals the supply voltage. One input may rise above VCC+ provided the other stays inside its common-mode span; no input may go below -0.3 V, or 0.3 V under VCC- on split supplies. Input current flows out of the pins and hardly changes with the output state."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Supply current is about 1.1 mA for all four comparators and changes little with supply voltage (1.3 mA typ at 30 V). Single or split supplies both work."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output short circuit",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The output current limits at roughly 20 mA independent of VCC+. A short from an output to VCC+, or several outputs shorted at once, can overheat and destroy the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interfacing",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The open-collector output, pulled up to the logic supply, drives TTL, DTL, ECL, MOS and CMOS inputs; one output can drive more than one gate input."
  }
 ],
